1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,140
Listen to a talk in a psychology class.

2
00:00:02,140 --> 00:00:04,640
Today we're going to talk about shyness

3
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:09,284
and discuss recent research on ways to help children learn to interact socially.

4
00:00:09,284 --> 00:00:11,070
Many people consider themselves shy.

5
00:00:11,070 --> 00:00:12,893
In fact, forty percent of people

6
00:00:12,893 --> 00:00:15,440
who took part in our survey said they were shy

7
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:18,060
that's two out of every five people.

8
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:22,230
And there are studies to indicate that the tendency toward shyness may be inherited.

9
00:00:22,230 --> 00:00:27,170
But just because certain children are timid doesn't mean they are doomed to be shy forever.

10
00:00:27,170 --> 00:00:28,898
There are things parents, teachers,

11
00:00:28,898 --> 00:00:32,140
and the children themselves can do to overcome this tendency

12
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:34,080
and even to prevent it.

13
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:38,736
One researcher found that if parents gently push their shy children to try new things,

14
00:00:38,736 --> 00:00:42,150
they can help these children become less afraid and less inhibited.

15
00:00:42,150 --> 00:00:45,270
Another way to help shy children is to train them in social skills.

16
00:00:45,270 --> 00:00:48,200
For example, there are special training groups

17
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:50,552
where children are taught things like looking at other children

18
00:00:50,552 --> 00:00:54,280
while talking to them, talking about other people's interests, and even smiling.

19
00:00:54,280 --> 00:01:00,097
These groups have been very successful at giving shy children a place to feel safe and accepted,

20
00:01:00,097 --> 00:01:02,150
and at building up their self-esteem.
